<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domus Aurea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">64–<a href="/years/68">68</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Golden House</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Domus Aurea, the Golden House, was a vast landscaped palace complex built by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, largely on the Oppian Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, after the great fire of 64 CE. His engineer-architects Celer and Severus nearly completed it before Nero's death in 68. Seen as a symbol of decadence, it was stripped of its marble, jewels and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and the Flavian Amphitheatre, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Baths</a> of Titus and the Baths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> were built on its grounds; the Oppian wing burned under Trajan in 104. Its buried frescoes, rediscovered at the end of the 15th century, inspired the grotesque decoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> artists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domus-aurea.jpg" alt="Domus Aurea" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rabax63 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EingangDomusAurea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Domus Aurea replaced and extended the Domus Transitoria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s first palace complex on the site. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, Nero took great interest in every detail and oversaw the engineer-architects Celer and Severus, who were also responsible for an attempted navigable canal meant to link Misenum with Lake Avernus. The complex covered parts of the slopes of the Palatine, Oppian and Caelian hills, with an artificial lake in the marshy valley. Its area can only be approximated: some scholars place it above 300 acres, others below 100.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pavilion on the Oppian Hill was designed mainly for entertainment, with 300 rooms and few sleeping quarters. Its eastern part had two principal dining rooms flanking an octagonal court under a dome with a central oculus, an early use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-concrete">Roman concrete</a>. Some scholars suggest the Octagonal Room served as a precedent for the Pantheon. The ceiling of a revolving dining room may have turned by a mechanism devised by Celer and Severus.</p><p>Ceilings were faced with semi-precious stones and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> veneers, and walls were frescoed in a different theme for each group of rooms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> placed mosaics in vaulted ceilings, where they had not been used before. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> names the painter as Famulus, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amulius">Amulius</a>, who worked only a few hours each day while the light was best. Nero also commissioned the bronze Colossus Neronis from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenodorus">Zenodorus</a>, which Pliny the Elder put at 30.3 m; it stood by the main entrance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>At the end of the 15th century a young Roman fell through a cleft in the Esquiline hillside into a grotto filled with painted figures. Young artists had themselves let down on ropes to see the grotteschi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> studied them as a revelation of the world of antiquity. Domenico Ghirlandaio, Martin van Heemskerck and Filippino Lippi left their signatures, as did later visitors such as Casanova and the Marquis de Sade. Four frescoes were removed in 1668.</p><p>Within about forty years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s death the buildings had been obliterated, which protected the wall paintings from moisture. Opening the site let in damp, humidity sometimes reaching 90 percent. The complex was closed at the end of 2005, partly reopened in 2007, closed in 2008, and part of a gallery vault collapsed in 2010. It reopened in 2014. The site has over 150 rooms, about a third open to the public, and a barrel-vaulted room with a sphinx was found in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domus Aurea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Great Fire of Rome in 64 CE, Nero seized much of it for his own domain and built the <b>Domus Aurea</b> there, with an artificial lake surrounded by pavilions, gardens and porticoes, and the gigantic bronze Colossus of Nero at its entrance.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
